"""kms-renderd - a minimal UPnP/DLNA MediaRenderer that drives kms-play.

============================================================================
WHAT THIS IS
============================================================================
A Digital Media Renderer (DMR, UPnP AV MediaRenderer:1) with no media server
side. A control point (BubbleUPnP, VLC, Home Assistant, a TV remote app) sends
SetAVTransportURI then Play, and this daemon starts the existing KMS player on
the URI it was given. Pause, Seek, Stop and the volume are forwarded to the
player's own control socket, so the three-plane zero-copy path, the OSD and the
subtitle plane are exactly the ones the player already had: this daemon never
touches DRM, GStreamer or the display mode.

    control point --SSDP/HTTP/SOAP--> kms-renderd --unix socket/JSON--> kms-play-py
                                                                         |
                                                              kms-subd + GStreamer

============================================================================
WHY NOT RYGEL / GMRENDER / UPMPDCLI
============================================================================
They own their GStreamer pipeline. Using one would mean giving up the
zero-copy NV15/NV12 plane (their sink is not kmssink with our plane ids), the
two ARGB planes that draw subtitles and the OSD, the decode-tier fallback and
the VT/exit handling - i.e. the whole point of this player. A renderer is a
small state machine plus two protocols, so it is written here, with the Python
standard library only: no new dependency, nothing to install.

============================================================================
SECURITY (READ THIS)
============================================================================
UPnP has NO authentication and no transport security: anything that can reach
the HTTP port or the SSDP socket can start a playback and seek. This daemon
therefore:

  * accepts SSDP M-SEARCH and HTTP requests only from private, link-local or
    loopback addresses (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16, 127/8,
    fe80::/10, fc00::/7, ::1). A request from a public address is refused and
    logged.
  * caps the HTTP body at 64 KiB and refuses chunked/oversized requests.
  * parses XML with xml.etree only, and rejects any document containing a
    DOCTYPE or ENTITY declaration, so no external entity is ever resolved.
  * never writes a file on behalf of a request, and never builds a shell
    command from a received string: the player is started with an argv list,
    the URI is one element of that list.
  * one player at a time, bounded timeouts on every socket, per-request
    try/except so a malformed request can never kill the daemon.

The port must NEVER be forwarded on the internet. There is nothing to gain and
everything to lose: a remote attacker could start playback on the screen.

============================================================================
EVENTING
============================================================================
SUBSCRIBE creates a subscription with an SID and answers with a Timeout
(BubbleUPnP and VLC are satisfied with that; they fall back to polling). This
version does not send NOTIFY state events: the state machine is small and
GetTransportInfo/GetPositionInfo are always live.

============================================================================
USAGE
============================================================================
    kms-renderd [--http-port 8200] [--friendly-name NAME] [--player PATH]

Environment: KMS_RENDERD_PLAYER, KMS_RENDERD_CONTROL_SOCKET (same default as
the player: /run/user/<uid>/kms-play.sock), KMS_RENDERD_HTTP_PORT.
"""

